Title = {The dynamic lateral gate of the mitochondrial β-barrel biogenesis
   machinery is blocked by darobactin A},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {16},
Number = {1},
Month = {NOV 20},
Abstract = {The folding and insertion of beta-barrel proteins into the mitochondrial
   outer membrane is facilitated by the sorting and assembly machinery
   (SAM) complex. Here we report two 2.8 \& Aring; cryo-EM structures of
   the Thermothelomyces thermophilus SAM complex in the absence of
   substrate in which the Sam50 lateral gate adopts two different
   conformations: the first is a closed lateral gate as observed in
   previously published structures, while the second contains a Sam50 with
   the first four beta-strands rotated outwards by approximately 45
   degrees, resulting in an open lateral gate. The observed monomeric open
   conformation contrasts our previous work where the open conformation was
   adopted by non-physiological up-down dimers. To understand how these
   lateral gate dynamics are influenced by substrate, we studied the
   interaction of the SAM complex with a beta-signal peptide mimic,
   darobactin A. Darobactin A binds to the SAM complex with nanomolar
   affinity and inhibits the import and assembly of mitochondrial
   beta-barrel proteins in vitro. Lastly, we solved a 3.0 \& Aring; cryo-EM
   structure of the Thermothelomyces thermophilus SAM complex bound to
   darobactin A, which reveals that darobactin A stabilizes the Sam50
   lateral gate similar to the open conformation by binding to strand beta
   1, therefore blocking beta-barrel biogenesis.},
